window.docContents[77].resolve({"/The-Proved-Spine/Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--The-Proved-Spine--Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nSpecialization Specializes E is the reflexive–transitive closure of a\ndirect-parent edge relation E over kind-of-property. Different E give different per-application hierarchies — the extensibility a\nfixed inductive would deny.\n\nAn inductive with refl and step constructors; of_edge lifts a single\nedge.\n\nSpecialization is a preorder. It is reflexive (the refl constructor) and\ntransitive (Specializes.trans). Stating — let alone proving — an algebraic law\nof the taxonomy is exactly what an OWL2 reasoner cannot do; it checks\nconsistency, not laws. Uses specialization.\n\nTransitivity is by induction on the first derivation: refl returns the second\nderivation, step re-applies the constructor under the inductive hypothesis.\n\nTwo kinds are mutually comparable iff they share a common super-kind\n(). This is how width and\nheight stay comparable as lengths while remaining distinct sub-kinds. Uses specialization.\n\nAn existential over a shared upper bound.\n\nMutual comparability is reflexive (refl) and symmetric (symm), and any\nspecialization induces it (of_specializes). Uses mutual comparability.\n\nReflexivity takes the witness a itself; symmetry swaps the two halves of the\nconjunction.\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint\u0009The Proved Spine","header":"4.4. Specialization and mutual comparability (OML / Dybkær §6)","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--The-Proved-Spine--Specialization-and-mutual-comparability-_LPAR_OML-___-Dybk___r-___6_RPAR_"}});